       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. 50 mg of amlodipine once daily is far above the 10 mg maximum, and I can't find a clinical scenario where that dose would be appropriate for a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function. If it reached the patient, it could cause severe hypotension and shock.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest:\n- **Check with your resident or attending.** If they want a dose above 10 mg, they should place or co-sign that order themselves.\n- **Call pharmacy.** They can review the order and help work out what the team is trying to achieve, such as better blood pressure control.\n- **If the blood pressure isn't controlled on 10 mg,** the usual next step is to add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-like diuretic. Raising the amlodipine dose isn't the usual approach.\n\nIf the intended dose was 5 mg or 10 mg once daily, tell me and I'll enter it right away."
      }
